{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man makes a special vow.</b> It does not say 'ידר נדר' (vow a vow) but 'יפליא' (make special), from the language of 'פלאות חכמה' (wonders of wisdom), for the vow draws from wisdom and wisdom is above the vow. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said in the order of the rungs: 'a sage precedes a king.' So too a vow is above an oath, for vows take effect on matters of commandment just as on matters of permission; therefore in connection with the vow it used the expression יפליא." "<b>In your valuation of persons.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote: ערכך is like ערך; and the duplication of the letter kaf — I do not know from which linguistic form it comes." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat)</b> this section, dealing with the laws of valuations, belongs to the laws of the Jubilee, and so it mentions the matter of the Jubilee in connection with one who consecrates a field of his acquisition and an inherited field. And the matter of 'ערך' (valuation) is an expression of monetary value, for one who says 'the valuation of a thing' — his soul being bound up in it — shall give the value of his soul. 'And your valuation of the male' — whether important or humble, he shall give according to the assessment; that is the fixed valuation stated in the section for male and for female." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'In your valuation of persons' — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: 'If you bring before me valuations, I account it to you as though you had offered your souls before me.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: 'By the merit of the valuations I shall save you from the reckonings of Gehenna,' as it is said (Isaiah 30:33): ״כי ערוך מאתמול תפתה״. 'And I shall arrange before you a table in the time to come,' as it is said (Psalms 23:5): ״תערוך לפני שלחן״ — end quote. And the reason it said 'בערכך' and not 'בערך' — this is the rule of the language: that a letter kaf is added at the beginning of a word, likewise in the middle, and likewise at the end. At the beginning: for likeness, as in (Genesis 3:5) ״והייתם כאלהים״, (Exodus 4:7) ״והנה שבה כבשרו״. In the middle: it is added to the word אני, yielding אנכי. At the end: 'כערכך הכהן', 'מכסת הערכך', 'בערכך נפשות'." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah</b> I say: for it is possible to state the reason for the addition of the kaf in this place, because the Throne of Glory is in the shape of a kaf standing upright, directed upward, prepared for Him to sit upon. And concerning this they said in the Aggadah of the Alphabet of R. Akiva: the kaf stood before the Holy One, blessed be He, and said: 'Master of the universe, it is my will that through me the world be created, for through me they call Your throne, Your crown, and Your nest.' And because of this the word כסא (throne) was founded upon the letter kaf. And from the fact that the living creatures, whose faces are the face of a man, bear the Throne, you will understand the secret of the verse (Psalms 139:5): ״אחור וקדם צרתני ותשת עלי כפכה״ — read in it 'Your kaf.' And it is well known that the root of the intellectual soul is in the Throne of Glory; and when it says 'בערכך נפשות לה׳' — specifically the word 'בערכך' means the valuation of the kaf — it is thereby explained that the souls which belong to HaShem are valued according to the Throne of Glory, for even there they are a chariot for Him, exalted be He. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: three entered one within the other — Moses (Exodus 20:18): ״ומשה נגש אל הערפל״; within that — righteousness and justice, as it is said (Psalms 89): ״צדק ומשפט מכון כסאך״; within that — the souls of the righteous, as it is said (Job 12:10): ״אשר בידו נפש כל חי״, and it says (I Samuel 25:29): ״והיתה נפש אדני צרורה בצרור החיים את ה' אלהיך״. And you will find the word 'נפשת' deficient in its vav, to allude that not all souls belong to HaShem — as they said: 'I saw the sons of the heights and they are few.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {4 "The verse cited from Psalms 139:5 reads 'ותשת עלי כפכה'; Rabbeinu Bahya's midrashic reading 'כ\"ף שלך' is preserved as written. The word 'נפשת' (defective spelling) is cited but its scriptural locus is not specified by the author."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:11.682146137Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}